.TH CHASHMAP_BEGIN_ITER 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chashmap_begin_iter \- create an iterator positioned at a hash map's first entry
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chashmap.h>

cmap_iterator *chashmap_begin_iter(chmap chm, char **err);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Allocates an iterator positioned at the map's first entry. Each step
gives the key and the value of one entry, which the caller reads with
the unified accessors ccol_iter_key_ptr(3) and ccol_iter_val_ptr(3).
The value pointer points at the map's own storage, so a write through
it updates the stored value in place.

The generic ccol_begin(3) macro selects this function for a chmap, and
ccol_begin(3) is the ordinary entry point. Call this function directly
only when the container type is already known and the caller wants the
error string.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
chm   Hash map to iterate over, or NULL
err   Optional pointer that receives an error string on failure, or NULL
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns a pointer to a newly allocated iterator positioned at the first
entry.
Returns NULL with *err set to NULL when there is nothing to iterate
(chm is NULL or the map holds no entries).
Returns NULL with *err set to an error string when the iterator itself
could not be allocated.
.fi
.SH "ITERATION ORDER"
.nf
The order follows the map's implementation strategy, which
chmap_create_full(3) describes. With either strategy the order is not
part of the map's contract, so do not depend on it.

Separate chaining keeps a list of every live entry and links each new
entry at the head of that list, so the iterator visits the newest
entry first and works back to the oldest one.

Open addressing has no such list: it walks the slot array from the
first slot to the last one, so the order follows where each key hashed
to rather than when the caller inserted it. The hash depends on the
mode of the map, and the keyed mode depends on a secret of the process,
so that order changes when the map switches to the keyed mode and then
differs from one process to the next. See chmap_create_full(3).
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The function sets *err to NULL at entry, before anything else. To tell
an empty result apart from a failure, test *err rather than the return
value
A NULL chm iterates as an empty map and does not report an error. This
is a deliberate cross-module contract that cvector_begin_iter(3) and
cbmap_begin_iter(3) share: the caller can iterate a lazily created map
field that is still NULL because nothing is in it yet, and no caller
needs to write its own NULL guard first
The iterator frees itself when ccol_iter_next(3) steps past the last
entry and returns NULL. An iteration that stops early must end with
ccol_iter_destroy(3); alternatively, the caller can declare the
iterator with ccol_iter_declare(3), so that scope exit destroys it
The iterator is allocated through the map's own memory management
procedures. This is why chmap_destroy_with_dtor(3) is the better way
to destroy a map whose values need a destructor for each entry: that
function walks the map with no allocation at all, while an iterator
that does the same work can fail under memory pressure
An insert into the map, or a removal from it, invalidates an open
iterator; a change of a value in place through ccol_iter_val_ptr(3)
does not
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
chmap_construct(scores, char *, int);
int score = 42;
chmap_insert(scores, "alice", score);

char *err = NULL;
ccol_iter_declare(scores, it);
for (it = chashmap_begin_iter(scores, &err); it; it = ccol_iter_next(it)) {
    printf("%s = %d\en", *ccol_iter_key_ptr(it),
                         *ccol_iter_val_ptr(it));
}
if (err) {
    fprintf(stderr, "cannot iterate: %s\en", err);
}
chmap_destroy(scores);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_begin (3),
.BR ccol_iter_next (3),
.BR ccol_iter_destroy (3),
.BR ccol_iter_declare (3),
.BR chmap_destroy_with_dtor (3),
.BR cbmap_begin_iter (3),
.BR cvector_begin_iter (3)
